course: everyday
lesson: 205
title: Pet care, food and responsibility
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A pet depends on you for everything: the right food, the right warmth, shade, play and care for its whole life.
explain: Human food is not pet food: onions, garlic, chocolate, grapes, salt and the sweetener xylitol can make dogs and cats very ill, so a pet eats its own food.
explain: Reptiles such as bearded dragons and snakes cannot make their own body heat, so the lamp and heat mat an adult set up must stay on as set.
explain: A girl in Florida sees her dog chewing a sock and a packet of pills; she tells her mum at once, because the sock could block its gut and the pills could poison it, and the vet may need to help quickly.
explain: A pet is a responsibility for life: you learn about the animal before choosing it, introduce newcomers slowly, and never abandon it.
fact: If a dog eats medicine, a sock or anything it should not, you tell an adult straight away.
fact: Some swallowed things can poison a dog or block its gut and need a vet.
fact: Reptiles cannot make their own body heat and get ill if they are cold, so their lamp and heat mat stay on as an adult set them up.
fact: Onions, garlic, chocolate, grapes, salt and xylitol sweetener can make pets very ill, so pets eat their own food.
fact: A pet should come from a rescue centre or a good breeder, and you learn how to care for the animal before choosing it.
fact: Pets from bad sellers are often ill, and every kind of animal needs different care.
fact: A new baby or a new pet is introduced to a pet slowly, always with an adult watching, because animals can feel jealous or scared.
fact: Abandoning a pet is cruel and against the law; if a family really cannot keep it, a shelter helps find it a new home.
fact: In play, let a pet catch the toy sometimes, because play that always ends in frustration makes animals stressed or snappy.
fact: Small animals in cages and hutches can overheat and die quickly in the sun, so their homes are kept in the shade.
fact: A bored pet can become unhappy and destructive; toys, play, walks and new things to sniff keep it happy.
fact: After a pet dies, the family waits until it is ready before getting a new one, because a new pet does not replace the old one.
exercise: Your dog has just swallowed a sock. What do you do? | Tell an adult straight away | Wait to see if it comes out by itself | Give the dog lots of bread to push it down | Hide the other socks and say nothing
exercise: Why can a swallowed sock or medicine be serious for a dog? | It can block the dog's gut or poison it | Dogs cannot taste anything at all that they swallow | It only makes the dog a bit sleepy for an afternoon | Dogs always sick socks up safely
exercise: Why does a pet bearded dragon need its heat lamp? | Reptiles cannot make their own body heat | The lamp just helps it see | It needs the bright light to grow its new scales | Lizards are scared of the dark at night, like babies
exercise: Your snake's tank feels cold and the heat mat is switched off. What do you do? | Tell an adult so it is set up and switched on again as before | Wrap the snake in a blanket from your bed | Take the snake out to sit in front of the oven | Leave it, because snakes like the cold
exercise: Where is a good place to get a pet? | A rescue centre or a good breeder | Whoever sells it cheapest online | A stranger at a car park | Anyone with a box of puppies on the street
exercise: What should your family do before choosing a pet? | Learn what that animal needs to be cared for | Pick the cutest one in the shop and learn about it later | Buy the biggest cage in the shop first, then the pet | Choose the cheapest one to feed
exercise: Which of these from your plate could make a dog very ill? | Grapes | Plain cooked carrot | Its own dog biscuits | Fresh water from its bowl
exercise: Your cat begs at dinner, which has onion and garlic in it. What do you do? | Feed it its own cat food instead | Give it a small piece as a treat | Wash the sauce off and give it some | Give it some only on special days
exercise: Your family brings home a new kitten, and you already have a dog. How do they meet? | Slowly, with an adult watching | Put them in one room and let them sort it out | Keep the kitten in the dog's bed | Let the dog chase it to make friends
exercise: You are tired of feeding your rabbit every day. What is right? | Keep caring for it, and if the family truly cannot, find it a home through a shelter | Let it go free in the park | Stop feeding it until someone else does | Leave its hutch at a neighbour's door
exercise: You always pull the toy away just before your puppy catches it. Why is that a problem? | Play that always ends in frustration makes animals stressed or snappy | Puppies do not like toys at all | It makes the puppy grow too fast | It is fine, because puppies never get upset
exercise: It is a hot summer day and your guinea pig's hutch is in full sun. What do you do? | Move the hutch into the shade | Leave it, as animals like warmth | Cover it with a black plastic sheet | Give it a warm bath to cool down
exercise: Your dog chews the sofa when left alone all day with nothing to do. What helps most? | More toys, play, walks and new things to sniff | Shouting at it loudly every time you come home | Keeping it shut in a dark room all day long | Giving it less food so that it is calmer
exercise: Your old cat has just died. Your friend says: "Just get a new one tomorrow." What is wise? | Wait until the family is ready, because a new pet does not replace the old one | Get one the same day so you forget the old cat | Buy a cat that looks exactly the same | Never have a pet again in your life
test: A boy in Ohio finds his dog licking an open bottle of pills. What is the right first step? | Tell an adult at once, as the vet may be needed | Wait until tomorrow morning to see how the dog is feeling | Give the dog milk to wash the pills down more quickly | Put the bottle away somewhere and forget all about it
test: Which is true about leftover chocolate cake and dogs? | Chocolate can make dogs very ill | A small slice is a healthy treat | Dogs need some sugar every day | Only white chocolate is bad for dogs
test: Why do people keep a lamp over a lizard's tank all day? | The lizard cannot warm itself and gets ill when cold | Lizards need the light to read the labels on their food | The lamp keeps flies and other insects away from the tank | Lizards can only fall asleep in bright light
test: Your aunt wants a parrot because it looks pretty. What should she do first? | Learn what a parrot needs and find a good breeder or rescue | Buy one from the cheapest advert she can find online this week | Get the parrot first and read about it later | Choose a nice cage before thinking about the bird
test: A new baby comes home, and the family dog seems nervous. What helps? | Introduce them slowly, always with an adult watching | Leave the dog alone with the baby so it gets used to it faster | Send the dog away to live outside in the garden for good | Let the toddler next door play with the dog instead
test: Which is the kindest and lawful way when a family truly cannot keep its dog? | Find it a new home through a shelter | Drive it to the woods and leave it there to be free | Let it loose in a different town far from home | Tie it outside a shop and walk away so someone finds it
test: On a heatwave day, why is a rabbit in a sunny hutch in danger? | Small animals can overheat and die quickly | Rabbits get sunburnt, but only on the tips of their ears | The hutch wood will catch fire | The rabbit will grow far too much thick fur in the heat
test: Your cat scratches the curtains and seems restless after weeks indoors with nothing new. What is the most likely cause? | It is bored and needs play and new things | It is too warm and wants a bath | It has eaten too much healthy food | Cats always scratch curtains when they are happy
test: A friend never lets her kitten catch the feather toy. The kitten starts biting her hand. Why? | Play that never ends in a catch frustrates and stresses it | The kitten is naturally evil | Kittens hate feathers | The kitten wants to be left in its cage
test: dog just ate my sock!! | Tell an adult straight away, it may need a vet | It'll be fine, dogs eat socks | Give him another to balance it | Wait a week and see
test: can the dog have some of my choc | No, chocolate makes dogs very ill | Yes, a little bit is a nice treat for him | Yes, white chocolate's fine | Only on his birthday, as a special treat
test: hot day, rabbit's hutch is in full sun | Move it into the shade, small animals overheat fast | Leave it, rabbits love sun | Give him a blanket | Close the hutch to keep the heat out
test: lizard's lamp is on all day, can i switch it off to save power | No, reptiles can't make their own heat | Yes, lizards like the cold | Yes, at lunchtime | Yes, heat mats are enough at any setting
explain: A family's dog ate a bar of dark chocolate left on the coffee table; within hours it was shaking and being sick, and the vet had to treat it overnight.
explain: A boy's hamster had no fresh water over a hot weekend while he was away; when he came back it was very weak, and it died a few days later.
fact: Chocolate, grapes, raisins, onions and the sweetener xylitol can poison dogs, and some can kill them.
fact: Small pets can become seriously ill within a day or two without water, so water must be checked every single day.
fact: Lilies are poisonous to cats; even a nibbled leaf or a little pollen can make a cat very ill, so tell an adult and call the vet at once.
test: dog's begging for my chocolate, just a bit ok? | No, chocolate is poison for dogs | Yes, one small square won't hurt him at all | Only milk chocolate, never dark | Only on his birthday, as a treat
test: Who looks after the rabbit's water when Mia goes away for the weekend? | She must ask someone to check it every day | Nobody, rabbits can go a whole week without water | The rabbit can drink enough from its wet food | She can leave it a big bowl of milk instead of water
test: our cat ate a lily from the vase and is being sick | Tell an adult and call the vet straight away | Give her some warm milk and wait till she's better | Let her sleep it off in her basket | Wait to see if it happens again
test: did u hear the neighbour's dog ate a whole box of chocolates at Christmas and like exploded, they had to put it down. real lesson? | Chocolate poisons dogs, so tell an adult at once if one eats any | Dogs only get sick from dark chocolate, and only in really huge amounts | Give the dog lots of water and it'll be fine by morning | Dogs can eat chocolate if it's only a small box
test: apparently the hamster at school died cos someone left it in the sun for like 5 mins and it literally cooked. what's probably blown up? | The 5 minutes; but small pets really do overheat fast in the sun | That hamsters can die from heat | That cages are kept in the shade | That small pets need careful care
test: everyone's saying that boy's family dumped their puppy on the motorway. should u spread it? | No, it may be untrue; if worried, tell an adult quietly | Yes, so everyone at school knows how cruel they are | Yes, and post it online with his name and his photo | Yes, abandoning pets is illegal so it's fine to say it
exam: Identify which of the following foods is known to be poisonous to dogs. | Grapes and raisins | Plain cooked chicken | Dog food prepared for the purpose | Fresh water
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate action where a dog has swallowed medicine or a sock? | An adult is to be informed without delay | The dog is to be given milk and observed | The matter may be ignored if the dog appears well | The dog is to be made to run until it is sick
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the heating of reptiles kept as pets. | Reptiles cannot generate their own body heat and require a lamp or mat | Reptiles generate abundant heat and require cooling | Reptiles require warmth only during the summer | Reptiles may be kept at any temperature whatever
exam: Ascertain the proper course of action where a family is genuinely unable to continue keeping its pet. | To seek the assistance of a shelter in finding a new home | To release the animal in a park or forest where it may live freely | To leave the animal outside a veterinary surgery overnight | To keep the animal but cease feeding it
